IMPORT `roc-core`
IMPORT `roc-definitions`

-- Rules of Court 2021 (Singapore), G.N. No. S 914/2021, Orders 2 to 8, as at 5 November 2026 -- THE DUTIES (regulative rules, roc1).
-- The text is a plain-text rendering made by a converter script; it is not the official text, and nothing here has been checked against the official text.
--
-- The duties below are provisions in which the text names the bearer, the act, and either one period counted in days from one anchor or no time at all.
-- Each is written as PARTY ... MUST / SHANT ... WITHIN ... HENCE ... LEST ... and run with #TRACE.
-- This is NOT every duty in the range.  Other provisions that put a duty on a named party (for example O 2 r 3(2), r 4(1), r 5(1); O 2 r 10(3), (7), (9) and r 12(3), (8), (9);
-- O 4 r 4(2)(a), r 8(4), r 8(7); O 5 r 1(2); O 6 r 8(1), r 10(1); O 8 r 1(2), r 2(3), r 5(1), (3), r 6(1), (3)) are decision functions in their Order module only, and are not written as PARTY rules in this run.
-- The list in this paragraph is not exhaustive; NOTES.md ("What the text turns out to say", item 16) says the same.
-- THE CLOCK.  WITHIN counts days from the anchor the text names (the day the statement of claim is served, the day of the request, ...), the anchor being day 0.
-- The Rules' own count of a period (Order 3, rule 3), including the move of a last day that is a non-court day, is NOT in the WITHIN: it is in the layer-1 decision
-- function for the same duty (listed beside each duty below), which takes the dates and the court calendar.  Read the two together.
-- WHAT THE MAIN BINARY CANNOT DO.  #ASSERT <duty> EQUALS FULFILLED is refused on main ("Trying to check equality on types that do not support it"), so a trace
-- is checked by reading what it prints; the expected outcome is written in a comment beside each #TRACE, and the same duty is asserted as a decision function in
-- roc1-tests-order-N.l4.  WAIT UNTIL is spelled WAITUNTIL on main 1.6.109.
-- Where the text names no time, the duty has no WITHIN (nothing is invented); where it names no actor, there is no duty here (see NOTES.md, Jural relations).
-- LEST is BREACH, with the plain statement of the missed duty as its reason.  Where the text names a consequence of default that is another party's power
-- (O 6 r 6(5) and r 7(7): the claimant may apply for judgment), that power is a decision function in layer 1, not a LEST here.

-- o 6 r 6(1): duty decision function in layer 1: `o 6 r 6(1) the notice is filed and served within 14 days after the statement of claim is served` (day 0 = the day the statement of claim is served on the defendant)
DECLARE `o 6 r 6(1) the party` IS ONE OF `o 6 r 6(1) defendant who is served an originating claim in Singapore`
DECLARE `o 6 r 6(1) the act` IS ONE OF `o 6 r 6(1) file and serve a notice of intention to contest or not contest`

-- src:524 | 6. —(1) A defendant who is served an originating claim in Singapore must file and serve a notice of intention to contest or not contest within 14 days
-- src:524 | after the statement of claim is served on the defendant.
@ref O 6 r 6(1) (src:524)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `o 6 r 6(1) the party` `o 6 r 6(1) the act`
`o 6 r 6(1) the duty` MEANS
    PARTY `o 6 r 6(1) defendant who is served an originating claim in Singapore`
    MUST `o 6 r 6(1) file and serve a notice of intention to contest or not contest`
    WITHIN 14
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BECAUSE "the notice of intention to contest or not contest was not filed and served within 14 days after the statement of claim was served on the defendant"

-- on day 14 (the last day): expected FULFILLED
#TRACE `o 6 r 6(1) the duty` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `o 6 r 6(1) defendant who is served an originating claim in Singapore` DOES `o 6 r 6(1) file and serve a notice of intention to contest or not contest` AT 14

-- on day 15 (one day late): expected BREACH
#TRACE `o 6 r 6(1) the duty` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `o 6 r 6(1) defendant who is served an originating claim in Singapore` DOES `o 6 r 6(1) file and serve a notice of intention to contest or not contest` AT 15

-- nothing done by day 14: still owed, expected the duty printed back with WITHIN 0
#TRACE `o 6 r 6(1) the duty` AT 0 WITH
    WAITUNTIL 14

-- nothing done and day 15 reached: expected BREACH
#TRACE `o 6 r 6(1) the duty` AT 0 WITH
    WAITUNTIL 15

